`config.LUTMetadata` should support an optional `color_mode` field. When `LUTMetadata.to_dict()` is called, all existing metadata fields must continue to serialize as before, and `color_mode` should be included when it is not `None`. When `LUTMetadata.from_dict()` is called, it should restore `color_mode` from the input dictionary if present, while remaining backward-compatible with old metadata dictionaries that do not contain this field.

`utils.lut_manager.LUTManager.save_keyed_json()` and `LUTManager._load_keyed_json()` should preserve `metadata.color_mode` through a save/load round trip for keyed JSON LUT files. A keyed JSON LUT with a top-level `"color_mode"` value such as `"4-Color (RYBW)"`, `"6-Color (RYBW 1296)"`, `"5-Color Extended"`, or `"8-Color Max"` should load back with the same `LUTMetadata.color_mode`. Older keyed JSON files without `"color_mode"` should still load successfully and should leave `metadata.color_mode` as `None`.

`LUTManager.infer_default_metadata(display_name, file_path, color_count, ...)` should be able to produce metadata whose `color_mode` reflects the actual color system being generated, not just a filename/count guess. Callers that already know the mode, such as merge endpoints, need a way to pass that mode through so the saved LUT JSON records it. In particular, the extractor merge behavior for 5-color extended and 8-color LUTs should save keyed JSON metadata with `"color_mode": "5-Color Extended"` and `"color_mode": "8-Color Max"` respectively.

`LUTManager._infer_color_mode_from_json(file_path)` should prefer an explicit top-level `"color_mode"` string in keyed JSON files over any inference. If no explicit mode exists, it should remain backward-compatible with old JSON LUT formats. For keyed JSON dictionaries, it should inspect the `"entries"` count and `"palette"` contents. Counts with unambiguous meanings should continue to map as follows: 32 entries means `"BW (Black & White)"`, 2468 entries means `"5-Color Extended"`, and 2738 entries means `"8-Color Max"`. Counts of 1024 and 1296 are ambiguous and must not be inferred solely by count. For a 1024-entry keyed JSON LUT, a palette containing Red/Blue style color names should infer `"4-Color (RYBW)"`, while a palette containing Cyan/Magenta style color names should infer `"4-Color (CMYW)"`. For a 1296-entry keyed JSON LUT, a palette containing Red/Blue style color names should infer `"6-Color (RYBW 1296)"`; otherwise a CMYW-style palette should infer `"6-Color (Smart 1296)"`.

The palette inspection should work with the keyed JSON palette represented either as a dictionary keyed by color names, for example `"palette": {"White": {...}, "Red": {...}, "Blue": {...}}`, or as a list of objects containing a `"color"` field. Matching should be case-insensitive.

Flat-list JSON LUTs, where the top level is an array and no palette metadata is available, should continue to use only unambiguous size-based inference. A 32-entry flat-list JSON should infer `"BW (Black & White)"`. A 1024-entry flat-list JSON should not guess CMYW or RYBW and should fall back to `"Merged"` because the variant cannot be determined without palette metadata.
